# Time Travel

The Time Travel section fundamentally redefines in-game causality by mapping temporal mechanics to version control concepts like branching, merging, and rebasing. Rather than attempting to simulate every atom of a world, the proposed engine relies on the "lazy evaluation" of reality. If an event or entity is not explicitly recorded in the database's event log, it does not exist, meaning players can interact with the past without immediately fracturing the timeline.

## Ruleset

This ruleset follows a branching paradigm of backward time travel: Every action made by a traveler that would change an established fact in the timeline forces a new branch on the parent timeline. The new branch shares the history of the parent branch; all new actions on the new branch are recorded independant of the parent branch. Events after the divergence point do not cross over.

Classic time travel paradoxes, such as the grandfather paradox, cease to be logical contradictions under this descendant branching model; you cannot kill your own grandfather, preventing your own birth, because your grandfather exists on a different branch. In this model, killing your grandfather means killing the new branch's "you"'s grandfather.

**Rules:**

* Because the event log only records established facts, minor actions in the past do not trigger a timeline branch unless they explicitly contradict an established "Anchor Entity".

    > **(Note)** Explore the concept of `anchor entity`. Gemini invented this.

* A time traveler cannot alter the history from which they originated.
* Any causal change to a traveler's established history necessarily produces a new descendant branch, such as moving from the "Main" timeline to a "Prime" timeline.

* Entities, physical objects, and knowledge are treated equally and carry their causal provenance with them when moving across branches.
* Database conflicts created by cross-branch travel are resolved using a composite primary key consisting of the entity's UUID and its origin branch ID.
    > **(Note)** It was debated whether or not branch ID should be included in the UUID.
* When identical entities from different branches exist in the same space, the LLM takes over the clone as an autonomous agent to resolve the paradox narratively rather than crashing the database.
    > **(Proposed)** The role of LLMs as in-game agents leads to intriguing possibilities and needs further discussion.

