### Consequences of Lazy Evaluation

Our proposal says that a branch is created only when a causal conflict occurs. It does not happen at the moment the time traveler arrives at their destination. This allows them to continue on the same branch, *so long as they make no changes that propogate through the already-established casaul chain.*

This is allowed through the lazy evaluation and creation of items, characters, locations, and their causal connections.

In our engine, an item only comes into existence when the LLM imagines it, decides it is important, and logs it to the database. Before this point, there is no casaul chain to it. In a sense, the LLM breathes life into it.

#### The Butterfly Effect, Squished Bugs, & Coffee

Imagine an engine that simulates everything. Every item in the universe, every atom they are composed of, and every casaul relationship thereof. A time traveler that arrives in the past cannot hope to make any action without disturbing the casual chain. This is the classic **Butterfly Effect**. which states that a small, seemingly insignificant change in the initial conditions of a complex, interconnected system can lead to dramatically large and unpredictable outcomes later on.

We find an example of this in the television show *The Simpoons*, where in one episode Homer creates a time machine and travels to the time of the dinosaurs. Here, in a flashback, Homer receives a stern warning from Grandpa Simpson not to step on or touch anything, because even the tiniest change will alter the future. Right after promising to stay still, a prehistoric mosquito flies by. Homer says, "Stupid bug! You go squish now!" and kills it. When Homer returns, he faces a now-changed, dystopian world. All the result of the squished bug.

We use the *cup of coffee* example to explore this concept by presenting the question: Can the player travel back in time, drink a cup of coffee, and return to the present without having upset causality? In our engine's lazy evaluation model, we find the answer to be yes, they can. In an engine that models literally everything, we find it to be impossible.

Consider first the engine that models everything. The player arrives in the past and pours their coffee. In the process, they sit down in a wooden chair, pour the last cup from the pot, drink, and leave back to their own time.

Let's consider now all of these objects. Because they and everything else have already been recorded down the causul line, even these simple actions necitate the creation of a new branch. Perhaps the chair being out of place causes someone to trip and hit their head, perhaps the missing coffee causes a very grumpy orc to go on a rampage. Everything the player has interacted with would have ripples down the timeline.

Now consider our lazy evaluation model. The sequence of actions is the same. The player tells the LLM "I sit down and have a cup of coffeee." The LLM fabricates the coffee, adds it into the event log as something that has occured (which is fundamentaly indistinct from other entries), and there is no casaul chain to disturb — because there was never one to begin with. The grumpy orc cannot go on a rampage, because it never knew of the existence of the coffee to begin with.

In lazy evaluation, it is entirely possible for two travelers to meet at a designated time and place, enjoy a cup of mocha, and part ways, with none being the wiser.

